---
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="Insurance Claim Resources - Public Adjuster Guides & Terms | Claim Guardians"
  description="Insurance claim resources and guides from licensed public adjusters. Understand policy terms, the claims process, and how to maximize your property damage settlement."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json">
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What is a Public Adjuster in Florida?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "A public adjuster is a state-licensed insurance professional. Property owners hire public adjusters to represent their financial interests. Claim Guardians evaluates your property damage, files the complex paperwork, and forces the insurance company to pay your maximum settlement. We work exclusively for you, not the insurer."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Is Claim Guardians licensed in Florida?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Yes. Claim Guardians holds active public adjusting licenses from the Florida Department of Financial Services (FLDFS). Our team strictly follows Florida state insurance regulations. Clients can verify our active credentials and state licenses directly through the official FLDFS website."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Why should I trust Claim Guardians with my property claim?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Claim Guardians employs state-certified property damage experts. Our adjusters leverage deep Florida insurance industry experience. We provide transparent communication, document every piece of hidden damage using advanced technology, and fight aggressively against unfair insurance company tactics to protect your financial future."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How does the Claim Guardians process work?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Claim Guardians simplifies the insurance claim process into three steps. First, our experts inspect your property for free. Second, our team files your claim and negotiates directly with the insurance adjuster. Finally, you receive your maximum financial settlement check."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How long does a property damage claim take in Florida?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Claim duration depends on the damage severity and the insurance company's response time. Claim Guardians expedites the process by submitting accurate, undeniable damage documentation from day one. We consistently pressure the insurance company to meet Florida’s strict statutory payment deadlines."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Can I hire Claim Guardians if my insurance claim was denied?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Yes. Property owners frequently hire Claim Guardians to appeal denied or underpaid insurance claims. Our licensed adjusters reopen closed claims, provide new structural evidence, and demand fair compensation from the insurance company. We routinely reverse unfair claim denials."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "How much does a public adjuster cost in Florida?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Claim Guardians charges $0 in upfront fees. Clients pay a contingency fee only after they receive their settlement check. Florida state law legally caps public adjuster fees at 20% for standard claims and 10% during declared state emergencies."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Do public adjuster fees reduce my final insurance payout?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Claim Guardians deducts the contingency fee from your final settlement. However, our experts typically negotiate significantly higher payouts than the initial insurance offers. Most clients receive more net money by using our services than by fighting the insurance company alone."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Will hiring a public adjuster get my insurance policy canceled?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "No. Florida law protects your right to hire a public adjuster. Insurance companies cannot legally cancel your policy simply because you hired professional representation. Claim Guardians protects your rights and ensures the insurer honors your policy contract."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What areas in Florida does Claim Guardians serve?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Claim Guardians serves property owners across the entire state of Florida. Our local adjusters quickly respond to claims in Miami, Fort Lauderdale, Orlando, Tampa, and Fort Myers. We dispatch experts immediately after hurricanes, fires, or sudden water damage events."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What should I do immediately after property damage?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Property owners must stop the source of damage and document the scene with photos. Next, you should contact Claim Guardians before calling your insurance company. Our adjusters will guide your initial steps to prevent accidental claim denials or recorded misstatements."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <!-- 1. Hero Section -->
  <PageHero title={"Property claim resources."} emphasis={"A little more clarity."} eyebrow={"Guides / Questions / Insurance terms"} description={"Find explanations, preparation guides and questions to bring to a claim review. Search the resources below or choose a topic."} current={"Resources"} ctaLabel={""}><div data-resource-search hidden class="relative max-w-2xl"><label for="resource-search" class="block mb-3">Search questions and guides</label><input type="search" id="resource-search" aria-controls="resource-content" placeholder="Search insurance terms, guides, or questions..." class="w-full px-4 py-4" /></div></PageHero>

  <!-- 2. Knowledge Categories -->
  <section class="section-white">
    <div class="section-inner relative z-20">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <!-- Card 1 (Guides) -->
        <a href="/resources/claim-review-guide/" class="card-hover p-8 md:p-12 group">
          <div class="text-5xl mb-6">📚</div>
          <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-[#082142] mb-4 group-hover:text-[#00808C] transition-colors">Claim Survival Guides</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 font-medium">A practical checklist to prepare your documents for a property claim review.</p>
        </a>

        <!-- Card 2 (Glossary) -->
        <a href="#glossary" class="card-hover p-8 md:p-12 group">
          <div class="text-5xl mb-6">📖</div>
          <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-[#082142] mb-4 group-hover:text-[#00808C] transition-colors">Insurance Glossary</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 font-medium">Plain-English definitions of complex insurance vocabulary used to confuse property owners.</p>
        </a>

        <!-- Card 3 (Comparisons) -->
        <a href="#adjuster-comparison" class="card-hover p-8 md:p-12 group">
          <div class="text-5xl mb-6">⚖️</div>
          <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-[#082142] mb-4 group-hover:text-[#00808C] transition-colors">Who Does an Adjuster Represent?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 font-medium">The critical difference between public adjusters and your insurance company's "staff" adjusters.</p>
        </a>

        <!-- Card 4 (Legal Rights) -->
        <a href="#licensing" class="card-hover p-8 md:p-12 group">
          <div class="text-5xl mb-6">🛡️</div>
          <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-[#082142] mb-4 group-hover:text-[#00808C] transition-colors">Licensing & Trust</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 font-medium">Learn about professional licensing and choosing a public adjuster.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div id="resource-content">
  <section class="section-muted">
    <div class="section-inner max-w-4xl space-y-8">
      <p id="search-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="text-slate-700"></p>
      <div id="search-empty" hidden class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p>No matching questions or guides. Try another term, or <a href="/contact/" class="underline text-[#006775]">contact our team</a>.</p>
        <button id="search-clear" type="button" class="btn-secondary mt-4">Show all resources</button>
      </div>
      <article id="claim-guide" data-search-item class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
        <h2 class="section-heading mb-4">Prepare for a property claim review</h2>
        <p class="mb-4"><a class="underline text-[#006775]" href="/resources/claim-review-guide/">Read the complete guide</a> · <a lang="es" class="underline text-[#006775]" href="/es/guia-reclamacion/">Leer en español</a></p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-slate-700">
          <li>Gather your insurance policy, declarations page, and claim number if you have one.</li>
          <li>Keep dated photos of the damage, taken only from areas you can access safely.</li>
          <li>Collect inspection reports, repair estimates, receipts, and an inventory of damaged items.</li>
          <li>Bring any insurer letters, offers, or denial explanations, and a short timeline of what happened.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600">Missing a document? You can still request a review and tell us what you have.</p>
      </article>
      <article id="glossary" data-search-item class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
        <h2 class="section-heading mb-4">Insurance glossary</h2>
        <dl class="space-y-4 text-slate-700">
          <div><dt class="font-bold">Declarations page</dt><dd>A summary of the policy, insured property, coverage limits, and deductibles.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-bold">Deductible</dt><dd>The amount the policy makes your responsibility for a covered loss. The applicable amount depends on the policy and loss.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-bold">Coverage limit</dt><dd>The maximum payable for a particular coverage, subject to the policy terms.</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="font-bold">Estimate</dt><dd>An assessment of the work and cost to repair damage. It is not a guarantee of insurance payment.</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <article id="adjuster-comparison" data-search-item class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
        <h2 class="section-heading mb-4">Who does an adjuster represent?</h2>
        <p class="text-slate-700 mb-3">A staff adjuster is employed by an insurer. An independent adjuster may be engaged by an insurer to assess a claim. A public adjuster is hired by the policyholder to represent the policyholder in the claim process.</p>
        <p class="text-slate-700">Before engaging a professional, check their license and ask for a written explanation of their role, services, and fees.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

